    <section id="why-gms">
      <h2>Why GMs And Creators Choose Library Packs</h2>
      <p>
        Library Packs are built for practical table prep, not passive browsing.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Faster prep:</strong> install drag-and-drop VTT assets instead of rebuilding folders every session.</li>
        <li><strong>Campaign consistency:</strong> keep maps, tokens, and encounter kits aligned across your tables and Wyrlds.</li>
        <li><strong>Instant relevance:</strong> share assets other GMs can immediately drop into live sessions.</li>
        <li><strong>Creator leverage:</strong> publish once, keep using the same packs in your own games, and help the wider community.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section id="community-advantage">
      <h2>The Community Advantage</h2>
      <p>
        This is more than a catalog. It is a network where subscribers can upload rights-cleared images, package them as themed
        packs, and distribute them to other active tables.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Community output grows continuously:</strong> new dungeon assets, token bundles, and battlemap kits land as creators publish.</li>
        <li><strong>Install where you play:</strong> discover and install packs directly inside your active library scope.</li>
        <li><strong>Niche demand gets served faster:</strong> forest ambush packs, cave map assets, ship boarding kits, and other focused collections.</li>
        <li><strong>Campaign continuity:</strong> shared packs keep recurring dungeon map assets and tokens aligned across Wyrlds.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section id="vs-stores">
      <h2>Why It Beats Traditional Asset Stores</h2>
      <p>
        Traditional asset stores are mostly one-way transactions. Library Packs are built for two-way participation and direct
        in-platform usage.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Store model:</strong> browse listings and buy static packs.</li>
        <li><strong>Library Pack model:</strong> build your own packs, publish, and let others install directly in platform.</li>
        <li><strong>Store model:</strong> content pipeline is usually centralized.</li>
        <li><strong>Library Pack model:</strong> community creators continuously add niche, table-tested bundles.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        The result is a flexible ecosystem where DMs are both consumers and contributors, with less friction than rigid marketplaces.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="subscription">
      <h2>Subscription Keeps The Network Active And Useful</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Pro User:</strong> enables Library Packs in personal scope.</li>
        <li><strong>Pro Wyrld:</strong> enables Library Packs in Wyrld scope.</li>
        <li>Discovery/install/publish workflows are pro-gated to keep participation intentional and quality-focused.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>See plan details on <a href="/pricing">Pricing</a>.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="rights">
      <h2>Use Images You Have Rights To Use</h2>
      <p>
        You can upload and share any images you own rights to use. That includes original work, licensed resources, or team assets
        you are authorized to distribute.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>No marketplace gatekeeping is required to organize and share legitimate VTT map assets and tokens.</li>
        <li>Creators stay responsible for rights compliance on content they publish.</li>
        <li>Teams benefit from practical sharing without unnecessary platform friction.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section id="examples">
      <h2>Example Community Packs</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Fantasy Dungeon Asset Pack:</strong> room tiles, corridor maps, props, and generic enemy tokens.</li>
        <li><strong>Forest Battlemap Assets:</strong> day/night variants, ambush overlays, and wilderness token sets.</li>
        <li><strong>Cave Map Assets for VTT:</strong> tunnels, cavern pieces, elevation markers, and encounter objects.</li>
        <li><strong>Monster Token Pack:</strong> reusable fantasy creature tokens for fast combat setup.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        These are representative examples of reusable tabletop asset packs teams can build and share.
      </p>
    </section>

